-- title:
--   Algebraicity over A₀[j] inside full-level modular function fields
-- statement:
--   Let $q$ be a prime and $M'$ a non-zero natural number. Inside the Laurent series field $\overline{\mathbb Q}((\mathsf q))$ consider two intermediate fields over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$: `modularFunctionFieldBar M'`, the field generated over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ by the coefficientwise image of the full level-$M'$ modular function field $\mathbb Q$-generated by the divisor expansions `divisorExpansions M'`, and `fieldBar q M'`, the field generated over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ by the coefficientwise image of the function field `xHFunctionField` of level $q^2M'$ attached to the subgroup $H \le (\mathbb Z/q^2M')^\times$ which is the kernel of reduction $(\mathbb Z/q^2M')^\times \to (\mathbb Z/q)^\times$, i.e. the units congruent to $1$ modulo $q$. Assume `modularFunctionFieldBar M'` $\le$ `fieldBar q M'`. Let $A_0$ be a commutative ring and let $F_0$ be a subfield of `fieldBar q M'` containing the element $\hat\jmath$, the image in `fieldBar q M'` of the $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-coefficient Laurent series $\mathsf q^{-1}\cdot jNumQ$ expanding the modular invariant $j$, which lies in `modularFunctionFieldBar M'`; and let $F_0$ carry an $A_0$-algebra structure. Then every $x \in F_0$ is algebraic over the $A_0$-subalgebra $A_0[\hat\jmath]$ of $F_0$ generated by $\hat\jmath$, that is, $x$ satisfies a non-zero polynomial with coefficients in $A_0[\hat\jmath]$.
--
--   This is the formal counterpart of the classical statement that the function field of a modular curve is a finite, hence algebraic, extension of the field generated by the modular invariant $j$, here in the form of algebraicity over the subring generated by $j$ over an arbitrary coefficient ring $A_0$. It is used in the descent arguments [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.exists_subalgebra_drinfeldRing_iff_localization_formallySmooth_card_le_descent`](thm.html#ModularCurve.FullLevel.exists_subalgebra_drinfeldRing_iff_localization_formallySmooth_card_le_descent) and its variants for $q = 2$ and $q = 3$.
